  "query_preview": "(This evening through Friday)...\nIssued at 128 PM EDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nOverview.\n\nHigh pressure will dominate central Indiana weather into early next \nweek, driving a trend toward clearer skies and warmer temperatures. \nLow clouds will erode through Saturday morning, giving way to \nabundant sunshine and daytime highs reaching the lower 80s by \nMonday. A cold front will approach from the northwest late Tuesday, \nbringing the next likelihood of showers and thunderstorms before \ntemperatures cool back toward seasonal averages late in the week.\n\nRest of Today Through Sunday.\n\nSynoptically, central Indiana remains sandwiched between a strong \nlow pressure system off the East Coast and broad ridging across the \nGreat Lakes. This continues to keep surface and near surface flow \nnortheasterly across the area with high clouds from a system to the \nnorthwest streaming in aloft. These high clouds along with CAA near \nthe surface will help to keep temperatures cool through the \nremainder of the day with some signs that the northeastern counties, \nwhich started cooler, may struggle to rise out of the low 60s. \nTonight, temporal differences in cloud clearing will dictate minimum \ntemperatures; areas in the northeast where clearing occurs earliest \nwill drop into the mid-to-upper 40s due to effective radiational \ncooling, while western counties hold in the lower 50s. On Saturday, \nlower cloud cover combined with weaker CAA should allow for warmer \ntemperatures with highs more towards the low to mid 70s.\n\nThe broader synoptic pattern remains fairly similar going into \nSunday with the aforementioned low pressure system along the East \nCoast remaining largely locked in place while the ridging across the \nGreat Lakes and system to the west become less pronounced. Surface \nflow will remain northerly to northeasterly but will be light, which \ncombined with another day of abundant sunshine will keep \ntemperatures in the low to mid 70s.\n\nMonday Through Friday.\n\nThe extended period opens with weak upper-level ridge amplification \nacross the eastern CONUS as surface high pressure shifts toward the \nMid-Atlantic coast. Both deterministic GFS and ECMWF along with \ntheir respective ensemble suites depict strong low-level warm air \nadvection developing across the Mississippi and Ohio Valleys as the \nlow off the East Coast finally begins to push off to the northeast.\n\nSoutherly flow early next week doesn't look quite as robust as it \ndid in recent model runs which will keep highs more towards the mid \n70s vs lower 80s. This begins to shift towards Wednesday as the \nsynoptic pattern begins to shift. Late Wednesday into Thursday a \nprogressive upper-level trough traverses the Northern Plains and \nGreat Lakes. Deterministic guidance indicates a surface cyclone \ntracking northeast into the Upper Midwest, pulling a trailing cold \nfront toward central Indiana late Wednesday into Thursday which is \nabout 12-18 hours slower than previous model runs. \n\nStrong synoptic-scale forcing via PVA and a developing 35-45 kt low-\nlevel jet will foster moisture transport, elevating PWAT values \ntoward 1.50 inches. Ensemble consensus supports increasing PoPs \nbeginning late Wednesday for showers and scattered thunderstorms \nalong with a lower-end potential for a few strong to severe storms. \nPost-frontal cold air advection behind the frontal passage will \nbreak the warm trend, returning temperatures to near seasonal \nnormals in the upper 60s and lower 70s going into Friday.",
